## One token, then the next

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 only does one thing: given some text, it gives a chance to every possible next token.
Writing a paragraph is a loop around that: run the model, pick a token, add it to the end, and
repeat. Every chat app writes this way. Try it: each press runs the model once. Click any word in
the list to pick it instead of the model's favorite.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 only ever does one thing: given some text, it gives a probability for every possible next
token. That's the whole stack from Part IV, run once. Writing a paragraph is just a loop around it:

1. run the model on the text so far,
2. pick a token from its probabilities,
3. add that token to the end of the text,
4. repeat.

Every model in a chat app writes this way. Try it yourself: each press runs the full model once.
Click any word in the list to pick it instead of the model's favorite.

</At>

<GlassBoxGenerator
	title="Glass-box generator"
	presets={[{ trace: 'the-cat-sat', label: 'The cat sat on the' }]}
	branches={['the-cat-sat-bed', 'the-cat-sat-couch', 'the-cat-sat-ground', 'the-cat-sat-edge']}
/>

<At levels="simple">

Keep pressing *Add the most likely* and GPT-2 writes "The cat sat on the floor, and the cat was
still asleep." Go back and pick " bed" instead, and it gets stuck saying the same thing over and
over. Picking at random, weighted by the chances, mostly avoids loops like this.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Keep pressing *Add the most likely* and GPT-2 writes "The cat sat on the floor, and the cat was
still asleep." Go back and pick " bed" instead, and it gets stuck: ", and the cat sat on the bed, and
the cat sat on the bed…". Always taking the single most likely token often falls into loops like
this. Picking at random, weighted by the probabilities (the <GlossaryPopover term="temperature">temperature</GlossaryPopover> spinner from <ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />), mostly
avoids them, which is one reason most text generators sample instead.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Greedy isn't the likeliest text

Written out, greedy decoding takes the top token at each step, and sampling draws one from the
tempered probabilities $P_T$:

$$
x_{t+1} = \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{x} P(x \mid x_1, \dots, x_t) \qquad \text{or} \qquad x_{t+1} \sim P_T(\,\cdot \mid x_1, \dots, x_t)
$$

where $x_1, \dots, x_t$ is the text so far and $\sim$ means "drawn at random from". The best
token at each step doesn't add up to the best text, because a text's chance is the product of its
tokens' chances. After *The cat sat on the*, greedy takes " floor" (7.6%), and then a comma (26%):
about 2% for the pair. " edge" starts lower (4.8%), but " of" follows it 96% of the time, so
" edge of" has about 4.6%, more than twice as likely. Beam search keeps several partial texts at
each step to find likelier ones, but the likeliest texts are often the dullest and most
repetitive, which is part of why open-ended writing samples instead.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>

## Penalizing repeats

<At levels="simple">

Apps that run a model can also make a token less likely each time it has been used, a
**frequency penalty**. Here is GPT-2 continuing "The cat sat on the bed", always taking the most
likely token, with no penalty, a small one, and a big one:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Most model APIs also offer a setting aimed straight at loops. A **frequency penalty** lowers the
score of every token by a set amount for each time it has already appeared in the reply, so the more
a token has been used, the less likely it gets. A **presence penalty** is the flat version: one
fixed cut for any token already used, however many times.<Cite id="openai-chat-api" /> Here is GPT-2
continuing "The cat sat on the bed", always taking the most likely token after the penalty:

</At>

<PenaltyRuns />

<At levels="simple">

With no penalty, the 32 tokens use only 7 different ones. A small penalty breaks the loop. A big
one stops GPT-2 from using any token twice, even " the", so the cat sits on "her back".

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

With no penalty, the 32 tokens use only 7 different ones. At 0.5, "the cat sat on the bed" comes
round once more, then the loop breaks and 25 of the 32 are different. At 5, no token appears twice.
Even " the" is ruled out, so the cat sits on "her back". A penalty works on tokens, not ideas, so a
strong one also pushes out the small words that every sentence needs.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

Written down, with $z_i$ token $i$'s score and $c_i$ the number of times it has appeared in the
reply so far, the recordings above use

$$
z_i' = z_i - \alpha_f\, c_i
$$

with $\alpha_f$ the frequency penalty, and a presence penalty $\alpha_p$ subtracts
$\alpha_p$ once from every token with $c_i > 0$. The softmax and the pick follow as usual. How big
a penalty needs to be depends on how spread out the model's scores are, so 0.5 for GPT-2 isn't
the same as 0.5 for another model.

</At>

## No going back

<At levels="simple">

Each token is chosen once, and everything after it builds on it. The model never goes back to fix a
word. And every new token means one more full run of the model. Writing is the slow part, and it
costs more too: on Anthropic's price list, a token the model writes costs five times as much as a
token it reads.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" />

That doesn't mean it can't look ahead. When researchers traced a large model writing a rhyming
poem, it had already picked likely words to end the next line on before it wrote that
line.<Cite id="lindsey2025biology" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Each token is chosen once, and everything after it is built on top. The model doesn't write a draft
or go back and fix an earlier word. If it picks " bed", it has to make the best of " bed". And each
new token costs a full run through all 124 million of GPT-2's weights, so a 500-token answer means
500 runs. The prompt's tokens can all go through the model together in one run, but the reply comes
out one run per token.<Cite id="pope2022" /> Prices fit that: on Anthropic's price list, every
model charges five times as much for a token of the reply as for a token of the
prompt.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" />

Choosing one token at a time doesn't mean thinking only one token ahead, though. Researchers at
Anthropic traced Claude 3.5 Haiku writing a rhyming couplet whose first line ended "grab it". Before
it wrote the first word of the second line, it already held two candidate endings, *rabbit* and
*habit*. When they switched off *rabbit*, the model wrote a different line, ending "a powerful
habit".<Cite id="lindsey2025biology" /> The plan lives in the model's numbers; the text still
comes out one token at a time.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

A run costs about two operations per weight, a multiply and an add, so GPT-2 small needs about
250 million operations per token, and about 124 billion for those 500 tokens.<Cite id="kaplan2020" />
Rerunning the model on the whole text for every token would cost far more, since the text grows
by one token each time. Real systems keep each earlier token's keys and values from attention and
only run the newest token through the model; <ChapterRef to="caching" /> shows how.<Cite id="pope2022" />

</At>

## Knowing when to stop

<At levels="simple">

The loop has to stop somehow. It stops when the model picks a special "end of text" token, or
when it reaches a length limit it was given. The model never sees that limit, so it doesn't write a
shorter answer to fit: the reply just stops, even mid-sentence. To get a short answer, ask for one.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The loop has to end somehow. GPT-2's vocabulary includes a special token, `<|endoftext|>` (number
50,256), that means "end of text". When the model picks it, the program stops.<Cite id="gpt2-config" />
Otherwise, the program stops at a length limit it was given. Chat models work the same way
with their own end-of-turn token.

The length limit is the program's, not the model's. The model never sees it, so it doesn't plan a
shorter answer: if the limit comes first, the reply is cut off, even mid-sentence, and the API
reports that it stopped for length. To get a short answer, ask for one in the prompt
(<ChapterRef to="instructions-and-roles" />). The limit counts only the reply, including a reasoning model's thinking, and the
prompt and reply together must still fit in the context window.<Cite id="openai-chat-api" />

</At>

## Run it live

Everything above is recorded. To type your own beginning, pick any word, and change the temperature,
choose the Small model tier on the Models page, linked from the top bar. It downloads a compressed
copy of GPT-2 small, with the tier's three other small models and the code that runs them (about
419 MB), and runs it on your device. Nothing you type leaves your browser.

<RecapCard>

- A model only predicts the next token. Generating text is a loop: predict, pick, append, repeat.
- Each token costs one full run of the model, and once picked it is never revised.
- Always taking the top token can loop; sampling with a temperature mostly avoids that, and a
  frequency penalty makes used tokens less likely.
- Generation stops at a special end token or a length limit. The model never sees the limit, so a
  low one cuts the reply off.

<At levels="deep">

- Greedy takes the $\operatorname{arg\,max}$ at each step, which isn't the likeliest whole text:
  " edge of" (4.6%) beats " floor," (2%).

</At>
</RecapCard>

